WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.950
Speaker 1: Let's hear from a few more people
via the David Pakman show Discord, which you

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:07.160
can find at David Pakman dot com slash discord.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:14.850
We are going to start right now right away
with no delay with Dylan from Kentucky.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.840
Dylan, what is on your mind today?

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.310
Speaker 2: Hey, David.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.930
I've been a long time, listeners.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.670
First time I ever got to call and speak to
you, so I'd be a little shaky here.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.949
Speaker 1: You're doing great so far, my friend.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:28.699
Speaker 2: Thank you, friend.

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:34.760
So I think for a long time, it's sort of been
seen as as the linchpin for what might fix,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.370
you know, our democracy.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.829
Here is the issue of campaign finance reform.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:48.239
And I wonder, I would like to get your thoughts
on how that can and will relate to the upcoming

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:55.590
Supreme Court decision on more versus Harper
and thus giving.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:00.129
State legislators more power over our elections
in general.

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:05.140
And I would like to just mute and listen to
you talk about that for a moment.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:11.630
Speaker 1: So one of the things when people
call in and they say, David, sir, you know,

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.490
sometimes they have tears in their eyes and
they say, what do you think are the most important

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:19.500
issues facing American politics?

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.630
The list that I've typically given over the
last few years, as I say, campaign finance

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.840
is one such issue.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.880
Media coverage of politics is another issue,
and it's very much influenced by how we finance

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.880
elections.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:38.490
Third is how we run our elections, meaning
first past the post in most cases, and also

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:41.330
the Electoral College for the presidency.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.210
I would like to see that changed.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:50.150
And fourth is climate change in the sense
that if your city is underwater or the pollution

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.530
in your city is so bad that it's going to
be hard to be an activist for the issues that

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:59.270
we care about unless we deal with climate
change in a in a sensible way.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.380
And then sometimes we'll also talk about access
to health care, because if you're sick and

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.560
don't have access to health care, it's also
hard to be an activist.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.229
Whether you care about education or abortion
or whatever, access to health care would also

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.420
be another issue.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:15.870
I still stand by that, but I think that we
need to be much more sort of strategic when

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.610
we simply say campaign finance reform.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:25.209
I almost think it's too broad for some people
to understand what we mean.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:32.600
And so a much more specific but still understandable
set of ideas should be there.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:39.010
And I think within that, the most important
ones would be public financing of elections

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.879
rather than, you know, just unlimited spending.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:43.650
Whatever you can raise, you can spend.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:49.170
So public financing of elections would be,
number one, doing everything we can to eliminate

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:54.350
the ability of dark money to come in right
now, even though there are quite strict limits

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.489
in terms of contributions directly to candidates.

00:02:57.489 --> 00:03:02.290
I forget whether it's 2700 per cycle or exactly
what the number is.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.760
There is the ability to donate huge amounts
of money to PACs and the money can sort of

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.220
be laundered and you don't know who's spending
what, where the money's coming from in all

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.530
cases necessarily.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:17.690
So that's really where I would would try to
focus that conversation.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.340
And to be honest, that I know now I'm getting
a little bit off the exact question.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:29.549
I would like to see us the left become way
more specific and strategic with all of these

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:30.549
issues.

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:36.910
So like, for example, when we talk about climate
change or climate change, we should be more

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:41.970
specific in terms of what we're talking about,
because sometimes the things that are repeated

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.610
about climate change aren't really the biggest
concerns.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.830
There's a really good book called The Precipice
by Toby Ord, which both argues I recently

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:51.830
read the book.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:52.830
It's a couple of years old.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:57.170
It both argues climate change is a massive
issue that we have to deal with.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:01.549
And also some of the statements made about
it are a little bit hyperbolic.

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:05.940
For example, if climate goes, if it gets if
the climate gets warmer by two degrees on

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.550
average, we're going to starve because we're
not going to able to grow food.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:14.270
When you look into that, actually it being
a little warmer would probably make it easier

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.519
to grow food in more parts of the world.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:19.930
The issues are different ones and we should
try to be more specific.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:26.530
And all of these all of these issues, I hope
to some degree I'm answering the question,

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.749
Dillon, and I hope to hear from you again
very, very soon

